Proof for merge stride

For clarity, I'll denote by σ B the stride obtained from the method described, as we do not know at first if it is the merge stride. Reversing the results of my proof:

σ B ⋅ x = ( σ ′ B )   x = ( ∑ i = 1 m σ i ′ B i ) x = ∑ i = 1 m ∑ j = 1 n σ i ′   b j , i   x j = ∑ i = 1 m σ j ′ ∑ j = 1 n m o d ( ⌊ σ j σ i s ′ ⌋ , s i ′ ) x j

Now, assume T is mergeable. In other words,

∃ σ M : T ( x ) = σ M ⋅ x , ∀ x ∈ I s

Our goal is to prove that σ M = σ B .

Firstly, I'll point out that if strides are canonical (as in, s i = 1 ⟹ σ i = 0 ) they are unique.
Therefore, we can assume (without loss of generality) canonical σ M , which is thus unique.

From expanding their formulas, we know that for vectors ( 0 , … , 0 , x j , 0 , … , 0 ) , we have

σ j B ⋅ x j = ∑ i = 1 m σ i ′ ⋅ m o d ( ⌊ σ j σ i s ′ ⌋ , s i ′ ) x j   σ j M ⋅ x j = T ( ( 0 , … , x j , … , 0 ) ) = ∑ i = 1 m σ i ′ ⋅ m o d ( ⌊ σ j x j σ i s ′ ⌋ , s i ′ )

We also know that σ B behaves similarly to σ M for coordinates equal to 0 or 1.

x j = 0 ⟹ σ j B ⋅ 0 = σ j M ⋅ 0 = 0 x j = 1 ⟹ σ j B ⋅ 1 = ∑ i = 1 m σ i ′ ⋅ m o d ( ⌊ σ j σ i s ′ ⌋ , s i ′ ) ⋅ 1 = ∑ i = 1 m σ i ′ ⋅ m o d ( ⌊ σ j ⋅ 1 σ i s ′ ⌋ , s i ′ ) = σ j M ⋅ 1

Therefore, by linearity, σ B = σ M .

This proves that σ B is the only merge stride that can potentially merge two shapes.
