Example

Here's an example of using this to get a view of a transposed tensor, then flattening it and showing the views are not mergable.

Consider a tensor T of shape ( 2 , 3 ) stored in row-major order. The mapping from a 2D index ( i , j ) to its memory position is given by the affine function f ( i , j ) = 3 i + j . We can express this in vector form as
f ( x ) = A x + b ,

with
x = ( i j ) , A = [ 3 1 ] , b = 0.

The transpose T T is obtained by swapping the indices:
f T ( i , j ) = f ( j , i ) = 3 j + i .

Which can be represented in matrix form with the permutation matrix
P = ( 0 1 1 0 ) ,

so the transform
g ( x ) = P x

trivially yields the merged view
f T ( x ) = f ( P x ) = A ( P x ) = ( A P ) x .

Where
A P = [ 3 1 ] ( 0 1 1 0 ) = [ 1 3 ] .

Next, suppose we flatten T T (which now has shape ( 3 , 2 ) ) in row-major order (hint: these views will not be mergable). The flattening is an affine mapping:
h ( i , j ) = 2 i + j ,

or in vector form,
h ( x ) = Q x ,

with
Q = [ 2 1 ] .

We now ask whether there exists an affine mapping
L ( z ) = α z + β ,

with α ∈ Z (a scalar) and β ∈ Z , such that for all x ∈ Z 2
L ( h ( x ) ) = f T ( x ) .

In terms of our mappings, this means:
L ( Q x ) = ( A P ) x .

Writing L ( Q x ) = α ( Q x ) + β and requiring the equality to hold for every x , we equate the coefficients:
α Q = A P and β = 0.

Substituting the known values:
α [ 2 1 ] = [ 1 3 ] .

This gives the system of equations:
2 α = 1 and α = 3.

Here, we see no such α exists even without gaussian elimination, since 2 α = 1 implies α = 1 2 while the second equation requires α = 3 , there is no scalar α that satisfies both.

Thus, no affine mapping (L) exists that makes
L ( h ( x ) ) = f T ( x )

hold for all x .
